WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.622
Modifiers

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:05.390
An intro phrase describes the first noun after the comma.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:07.589
How to spot it

00:00:07.989 --> 00:00:10.146
A describing phrase, then a comma

00:00:10.696 --> 00:00:12.632
Choices starting with different nouns

00:00:13.182 --> 00:00:17.420
It’s testing: whether a description sits next to what it describes

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:19.415
The method

00:00:19.815 --> 00:00:22.429
Step 1: Read the intro phrase

00:00:22.709 --> 00:00:24.444
Who or what is it describing?

00:00:24.994 --> 00:00:27.245
Step 2: Put that noun first

00:00:27.525 --> 00:00:28.715
Right after the comma.

00:00:29.265 --> 00:00:31.463
Step 3: Watch possessives

00:00:31.743 --> 00:00:34.748
“Morrison’s novels” makes “novels” the subject.

00:00:35.298 --> 00:00:36.408
In a few words

00:00:36.558 --> 00:00:38.814
Who’s described? Put them first.

00:00:40.014 --> 00:00:40.836
The rules

00:00:41.236 --> 00:00:43.144
Intro phrase → first noun

00:00:43.424 --> 00:00:46.539
Wrong: Walking to school, the rain started.

00:00:46.819 --> 00:00:50.306
Right: Walking to school, I got caught in the rain.

00:00:50.856 --> 00:00:52.345
Whoever did it comes first

00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:56.671
Wrong: After testing the samples, a conclusion was reached.

00:00:56.951 --> 00:01:01.260
Right: After testing the samples, the researchers concluded…

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:03.445
Possessives shift the subject

00:01:03.725 --> 00:01:07.769
Wrong: Born in 1939, Carrera’s novels…

00:01:08.049 --> 00:01:12.377
Right: Born in 1939, Carrera wrote novels.

00:01:13.577 --> 00:01:14.618
Let’s try one

00:01:15.019 --> 00:01:22.884
Born in Guatemala in 1939, ______ wrote novels and essays that explored the experiences of Indigenous

00:01:22.884 --> 00:01:23.737
communities.

00:01:24.387 --> 00:01:29.281
Born in Guatemala in 1939 describes a person.

00:01:29.831 --> 00:01:34.250
The work wasn’t born. Her novels weren’t born. It wasn’t born.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.219
Margarita Carrera was born, so her name goes right after the comma. Choice C.

00:01:41.419 --> 00:01:42.414
Common traps

00:01:42.814 --> 00:01:43.792
The possessive

00:01:44.072 --> 00:01:46.737
The name is there, but it isn’t the subject.

00:01:47.287 --> 00:01:48.265
Passive voice

00:01:48.545 --> 00:01:50.343
“…was made by” hides who did it.

00:01:51.543 --> 00:01:52.564
Modifiers

00:01:52.844 --> 00:01:55.101
Who’s described? Put them first.

00:01:55.651 --> 00:01:56.423
Spot it

00:01:56.623 --> 00:02:00.981
Describing phrase + comma; choices start with different nouns

00:02:01.531 --> 00:02:02.238
Steps

00:02:02.438 --> 00:02:05.277
1. Who or what does the intro describe?

00:02:05.557 --> 00:02:08.041
2. Put that noun right after the comma

00:02:08.321 --> 00:02:10.333
3. Watch out for possessives

00:02:10.883 --> 00:02:11.635
Remember

00:02:11.835 --> 00:02:14.475
Intro phrase → first noun after the comma

00:02:14.755 --> 00:02:16.245
Whoever did it comes first

00:02:16.525 --> 00:02:19.599
Morrison’s novels → the subject is “novels”

00:02:20.149 --> 00:02:20.755
Trap

00:02:20.955 --> 00:02:23.213
The right name, but as a possessive

